s is the evidence from `personal_alternation_ideal`, the simplest of the current models. As the brief asked, the new idea is in the decision rule: each person also has their own lapse rate, the share of trials on which they don't weigh the sequences at all and pick a side at random. So people differ both in what they think randomness looks like and in how consistently they act on that view.\n\n**Model:**\n- The ideal switching rate and the lapse rate are both person-specific. Each comes from a population distribution and is set up so the sampler handles it more easily.\n- Each has 400 per-person slots, so the next experiment's design can predict new participants.\n- `p_left = 0.5·lapse + (1 − lapse)·sigmoid(β·(dist_b − dist_a))`, where each distance is the squared gap between a sequence's switch rate and the person's ideal.\n\n**Not yet checked:**\n- **Convergence:** a one-chain smoke fit can't report R-hat, so convergence wasn't verified. Admission's full fit will test it.\n- **LOO reliability:** the PSIS-LOO estimate was flagged as unreliable on the smoke fit (3.2% of trials had high Pareto k, above the 1% tolerance).\n- **Novelty:** this check doesn't cover it. A modest lapse rate could leave the predictions close to `personal_alternation_ideal`'s. In that case admission would reject the model as a near-duplicate.\n\nThe three files are in `experiment1/model_loop/iter_2/candidate_2/`: `hypothesis.md`, `model_name.txt` and `candidate.py`.","ttft_ms":5120,"type":"result","duration_ms":252952,"uuid":"52d9f47d-0278-484b-b2fa-858eb494028c","ttft_stream_ms":1390,"time_to_request_ms":96,"first_content_frame_ms":1391,"queued_turn_count":0,"result_index":0}
